## 7. Full Redis Auth Flow

### Register/Login Flow

```txt
User enters email/password
        ↓
API validates input
        ↓
MongoDB checks user
        ↓
Password is verified
        ↓
Redis session is created
        ↓
HttpOnly cookie is set
        ↓
User goes to dashboard
```

Redis stores:

```txt
session:abc123
```

Value:

```json
{
  "userId": "u1",
  "sessionVersion": 1
}
```

Browser stores:

```txt
roleforge_session=abc123
```

---

### Protected Route Flow

```txt
User opens /manager/dashboard
        ↓
Browser sends HttpOnly cookie automatically
        ↓
Server reads sessionId from cookie
        ↓
Server checks Redis session
        ↓
Server gets user from MongoDB
        ↓
Server checks sessionVersion
        ↓
Server checks role
        ↓
Allow or redirect
```

---

### Logout Flow

```txt
User clicks logout
        ↓
Server reads cookie
        ↓
Server gets sessionId
        ↓
Server deletes session from Redis
        ↓
Server removes sessionId from user_sessions
        ↓
Server deletes cookie
        ↓
User is logged out
```

---

### Suspicious Activity Flow

```txt
Request comes with session cookie
        ↓
Server reads session from Redis
        ↓
Server checks IP / user-agent / device pattern
        ↓
If suspicious:
        ↓
Delete Redis session
        ↓
Increment sessionVersion
        ↓
Logout all devices
        ↓
Send security email
        ↓
Force login again
```

---

## 8. Simple Interview Answer

Redis is better than plain JWT when we need strong session control.

JWT is stateless, so if it is stolen, it usually works until expiry.

Redis is stateful, so the server can delete a session immediately.

Suspicious activity is not detected automatically by Redis. It is detected by checking IP, device, location, failed attempts, active sessions, and unusual behavior.

Redis helps us control the damage by deleting suspicious sessions, logging out all devices, and combining with sessionVersion.


